Date: Thu, 16 Dec 1993 19:10:31 -0500
From: David Buchanan <dbb@virginia.edu>
Subject: Kids These Days

>I make it a habit to treat anyone over the age of 13 as an adult
>unless they show by their actions that I should treat them as
>children.  This age was worked out by many cultures over many
>centuries, and just because in the last hundred years we've gotten
>into the habit of thinking that 18 or 21 is 'adulthood' is no
>reason to slight younger but intelligent people.
>
>Dani Eder

My kids would appreciate your attitude.  I think that most children want to
and can become adults by 14 or 15 if they are allowed to do so.  I don't
mean financially self-supporting, because education is important and
expensive.  But they should be able produce income for their own
entertainment, transportation, and other needs, to choose their own
friends, hours, educational preferences, sexual activities, and so on.  Of
course, they carry the consequences of their choices.   

I am not arguing for indulgence.  Our method involved lots of parental
presence, nurturing, physical closeness when the kids were very young.  As
they grew they were allowed/required to do as much for themselves as they
could (it's often easier to do something for a child than to let them do it
for themselves).  When they were 14 it was clearly time to let them loose,
to trust them.

If you can't trust them by this age any attempt to affect their behavior
for the next several years is likely to be counterproductive anyway.  Scary
as it is for parents, teenagers are going to engage in some reckless
behavior.  But the most self-destructive behavior I have seen was by kids
who were attempting to punish their parents, often for not leaving them
alone.  If you foster independence and give them control of their lives in
the early teen years at least they will only be doing dangerous stuff
because it's fun.  (Maybe things like eschewing a perfectly safe cesarean
birth followed by hormone regulation for the ecstatic rush of a natural
birth.)

David






