There's an interesting article in Science about an experiment where
they covered half of the visual field of one eye of newborn chicks
with a translucent screen, and the corresponding half of that eyeball
became elongated.  The hypothesis is that the back of the eye grows
until there is a fairly high contrast at the retina; if you
artificially eliminate the contrast by covering the eye, it grows too
much.  Reading and looking at terminals all day would probably reduce
the contrast significantly, compared to wandering around outdoors all
the time, because the letters on the page/screen don't focus well
except at the foeva.

There are probably genetic factors too.  I vaguely remember reading
about that, but I have no reference offhand..

