From:

Duncan Frissell:

Some ignorant people have thought that video/audio
was more "real" and trustworthy than lying print.

>From this point on in history with special effects technology as
developed as it is, you will be unable to distinguish "real" video from
fictional video in any case.  Audio and video will be no more trustworthy
than print -- if they ever were.
.................................................

I thought the issue in question was whether one method of getting one's 
info was superior to the other, and whether there was a way to 'make 
up' the difference for those who cannot read, i.e., who have some 
difficulty with the abstractness of the symbols used in writing/reading.

It is possible to have difficulty understanding what one sees in front 
of one's eyes, whether it is real or fictional,  factually accurate or 
untrustworthy, but an un-symbolized sensory reception of anything (i.e. 
seeing the color blue, as compared to reading the word 'blue') provides 
information which even someone with limited intellectual capacities 
could apprehend and derive meaning from, thus simplifying the process 
of comprehension, eliminating the necessity of imagining what an 
intermediary translator/interpreter is attempting to convey through the 
use of abstract symbols.

Blanc


