Date: Thu, 16 Dec 93 18:46:22 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Holiday Greeting

    I sent a personal newsletter out with my holiday cards this
December.  Recipient Tim May thought enough Extropians might like the
letter that I should post it.  I hope he's right, and I promise
not to do things like this too often...

#####
                                               Winter Holidays, 1993
Dear Friends,

    Another consistent year for address and job.  My employer, MasPar,
hangs in there.  We're still not public.  But no more microcode for
me!  I now do diagnostics and other software.  I think that's an
improvement...

    I planted more roses -- I have over 90 in my yard -- and cut
nearly 4000 blossoms this season.  I also planted a tree, a dawn
redwood, _Metasequoia_.  This coast redwood relative was known only
from the fossil record, perhaps back to the great dinosaurs, until the
1930s, when someone found a stand of them in China.  They probably do
well in California, but no one knows -- there hasn't been time yet for
any of the ones here to reach full size.  I have a fossil
_Metasequoia_ leaf, some thirty million years old.  Foliage from my
tree fits it in perfect registration.

    One cat less, one cat more:  Pixie died November 16, of diseases
of age.  He had lived with me since 1980.  Button, Percival P.
Pussycat, Elinor and Emma Woodhouse ("Bean") survive him.  Adult stray
Peaches arrived in June.  He is an affectionate yellow and white tom
with feline immunodeficiency virus -- cat AIDS.  It isn't too
contagious (unlike feline leukemia), but he lives quarantined in my
bedroom anyway.  The disease is as lethal as human AIDS, but takes as
long to develop, and since cats have shorter lifespans than we do, it
is in a sense less serious.

    I continue in marine biology at San Jose State University.  In
spring I took biological oceanography at Moss Landing Marine Lab, from
a top researcher on single-celled oceanic algae.  These creatures
likely make most atmospheric oxygen, and are poorly understood.  He
dropped us into his work -- we did one-day expeditions on the
_Point_Sur_, a real oceanographic ship, dropped real instruments into
the real ocean, took real data and got real confused.  This was the
first real science I had done in years, and I found I missed it.  Then
I did some independent study with the same person, writing a computer
simulation of a simple model of a population of the critters.  I had
been hoping for positive synergy between my physics and
computer-science background and biology, and I hit pay dirt -- in less
than a week of my own time, I wrote an easy-to-use program that
produced results in useful form, and showed him things he was
interested in but had never seen.  Whee!

    This fall I also took upper-division vertebrate zoology.  We went
on fun field trips.  In one haul of a 30 by 1.5-meter seine off Half
Moon Bay, we got eight bat rays up to 0.9 meter wide, a small leopard
shark (0.75 meter long) and thousands of surf perch.  We all petted
the shark, then threw everybody back.  Next, while predator-calling in
the ranges southeast of San Jose, we drew a coyote to within 20 meters
of us, and also brought in a bobcat and wild pigs.  The latter reach
250 kilograms in this area.  And other trips got me interested in
birds...

    Bird-watching can be the down side of Rutherford's remark that
"All science is either physics or stamp collecting."  Yet bird
behavior is complex, and once you have learned a little, there is much
to see.  I've observed a scrub jay dig a hole in hard adobe in my back
yard, deposit an acorn there, cover it, and mark the spot with a sprig
of foliage.  Not bad for a brain the size of a thimble.  I've seen a
snowy egret seize a mouse, break its back with a shake of the bill and
swallow it whole, much as its uncle _Tyrannosaurus_rex_ did with a
larger victim in _Jurassic_Park_.  (My vertebrate zoology text says
birds are therapod dinosaurs, just like _T._rex_.)  And who would
expect wild top predators in suburbia?  Yet I've watched two crows
chase a rather vexed red-tailed hawk over my lot and harass it in a
nearby tree, and I see a bird of prey about every five minutes when I
drive on the freeway, even in winter.

    How odd.  My physics doctoral project was on interstellar matter,
and my neatest experiences as an amateur astronomer were visual
observations of distant quasars (3C273) and ghost-faint galaxies (the
Sculptor dwarf).  Yet now I seem to be discovering the Earth.  At
least I have a background that lets me see it in perspective:  We live
on a wonderful planet, that most people don't even know exists.

    Books (and other stuff) of the year:  Science fiction and fantasy:
Vernor Vinge, _A_Fire_Upon_the_Deep_; Pat Murphy, _The_Falling_Woman_.
Other fiction:  Martin Cruz Smith, _Red_Square_ (sequel to _Gorky_Park_
and _Polar_Star_).  Nonfiction:  Mark and Delia Owens,
_The_Eye_of_the_Elephant_.  Albums:  Loreena McKennitt, _The_Visit_;
Cirque de Soleil, _Saltimbanco_.  Movies:  _Jurassic_Park_ (which
finally displaced _2001:__A_Space_Odyssey_ as my favorite
science-fiction movie); _The_Secret_Garden_.

    And I close with a wish for happy holidays and a joyous new year
to you and yours, from

